{
  "dom_id": "step-q31646",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-4t+2\u27e9,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t+2)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83{t}^{2}-4t-1,4t-4\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e83(2)^{2}-4(2)-1,4(2)-4\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
